# Target C — bridge-subtracted outer-face isoperimetry

**Date:** 2026-09-13  
**Status:** proof-only finite candidate; theorem-state authority unchanged.  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The two-phase boundary charge leaves the term `2B_o`, where `B_o` counts graph
bridges that occur on the unbounded face. This note removes that term whenever the
same graph carries a near-isodiametric amount of bounded-face area. The result is a
finite planar incidence lemma followed by an asymptotic corollary.

It does not assert that a selected harmonic carrier has near-disk area, or that the
whole positive-contact graph is globally two-phase. Those are separate hypotheses.

## 1. Finite bridge-subtracted inequality

Let `G` be a finite connected straight-line plane graph with unit edges. Let `W` be
the occurrence-counted boundary walk of the unbounded face and let `L` be its length.
Let `B_o` be the number of graph-theoretic bridges whose two edge sides are incident
to the unbounded face. Such a bridge occurs exactly twice in `W`.

Let `U` be the union of the closures of all bounded faces. Decompose its positive-area
part into connected finite-perimeter components `U_1,...,U_s`; components that meet
only at zero-area sets are kept separate. Write

\[
A:=\operatorname{area}(U),
\qquad
p_j:=\mathcal H^1(\partial U_j).
\]

The perimeter includes every boundary component of `U_j`, including boundaries of
holes. Then

\[
\boxed{\sum_jp_j\le L-2B_o.}
\tag{BI.1}
\]

Consequently, by planar isoperimetry,

\[
\boxed{
A\le { (L-2B_o)^2\over4\pi}.
}
\tag{BI.2}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
\boxed{
B_o\le {L-2\sqrt{\pi A}\over2}.
}
\tag{BI.3}
\]

The right side is nonnegative by (BI.2).

### Proof

Every physical edge in the boundary of `U` has a bounded face on one side. Since
`U` contains the closures of all bounded faces, its other side is incident to the
unbounded face. Such an edge is not a bridge with the unbounded face on both sides.
Conversely, an outer-face bridge has no bounded face on either side and contributes no
edge to `partial U`.

Each non-bridge edge incident to the unbounded face contributes one occurrence to
`W`, while each of the `B_o` outer bridges contributes two occurrences. Therefore the
perimeter edge occurrences available to `partial U` are at most `L-2B_o`. Unit edge
lengths turn this occurrence bound into (BI.1). This remains true with articulation
points and repeated boundary vertices; the count is by edge occurrences.

For each finite-perimeter component, the planar isoperimetric inequality gives

\[
\operatorname{area}(U_j)\le {p_j^2\over4\pi}.
\]

Summing and using `sum_j p_j^2 <= (sum_j p_j)^2` proves (BI.2). Rearranging its
nonnegative square root gives (BI.3). `square`

An internal bridge with the bounded face on both sides is deliberately not counted by
`B_o`; it lies in the interior of the bounded-face union or on a zero-area seam and
does not consume an outer-face occurrence.

## 2. Near-isodiametric consequence

Suppose a sequence is scaled by a diameter parameter `D` and satisfies

\[
A_D\ge\left({\pi\over4}-\varepsilon_D\right)D^2,
\qquad
L_D\le(\pi+\rho_D)D,
\]

with `epsilon_D, rho_D -> 0`. From (BI.3),

\[
0\le {B_{o,D}\over D}
\le {1\over2}\left[
\pi+\rho_D-2\sqrt{\pi\left({\pi\over4}-\varepsilon_D\right)}
\right]
\longrightarrow0.
\tag{BI.4}
\]

Thus a near-isodiametric bounded-face union and a near-geodesic outer walk have only
`o(D)` outer bridges. The conclusion is false without both hypotheses: a graph can
have many outer bridges and either carry little bounded area or spend a linear outer
walk surplus.

The same finite formula gives the nonzero-surplus version. If
`L_D/D -> M >= pi` and `A_D/D^2 -> pi/4`, then

\[
\limsup {2B_{o,D}\over D}\le M-\pi.
\tag{BI.5}
\]

## 2.1 The full strict-rhombus route variant

The same incidence argument applies when the graph's entire bounded-face union is
replaced by the union `R` of its strict-rhombus faces. Let `A_4=area(R)`, and let
`P_R` be the total finite perimeter of its positive-area components, including hole
boundaries. Every edge on `partial R` has on its other side either the unbounded face
or a bounded nonquadrilateral face. An outer bridge has the unbounded face on both
sides and is not on `partial R`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
P_R\le (b-2B_o)+P_{exc},
}
\tag{BI.6}
\]

where `b` is the outer-face walk length and `P_exc` is the total degree of bounded
nonquadrilateral faces. Isoperimetry gives

\[
\boxed{
A_4\le{(b+P_{exc}-2B_o)^2\over4\pi}.
}
\tag{BI.7}
\]

In the Type-II full-core branch, `A_4=\pi D^2/4-o(D^2)`, so

\[
\boxed{
(b-\pi D)+P_{exc}-2B_o\ge-o(D).
}
\tag{BI.8}
\]

Equivalently,

\[
2B_o\le(b-\pi D)+P_{exc}+o(D).
\tag{BI.9}
\]

This is an all-detour coupling: outer bridges consume at most half of the same route
surplus that remains after the full strict-rhombus area is paid for. It does not bound
the positive route surplus itself.

## 3. Composition with the two-phase boundary charge

Use the notation of the proof-only two-phase boundary occurrence theorem:

- `N_o` counts outer-walk occurrences outside two selected projective directions;
- `P_exc` is the total degree of bounded nonquadrilateral faces; and
- `B_o` is the outer-bridge count.

That exact incidence theorem gives

\[
N_o\le P_{exc}+2B_o.
\tag{BI.10}
\]

Assume, in addition, that the two selected directions have sine `s`, the outer-walk
convex hull has both selected reciprocal widths `D-o(D)`, and `L_D/D -> M`. The
projection variation argument gives

\[
{N_o\over D}\ge {4-sM\over2-s}-o(1).
\tag{BI.11}
\]

If the **full bounded-face union** also satisfies `A_D/D^2 -> pi/4`, then (BI.5)
and (BI.10) imply the all-detour necessary payment

\[
\boxed{
{P_{exc}\over D}+M-\pi
\ge {4-sM\over2-s}-o(1).
}
\tag{BI.12}
\]

At the near-geodesic endpoint `M=pi`, this reduces to

\[
\boxed{
P_{exc}\ge(4-\pi)D-o(D).
}
\tag{BI.13}
\]

If the graph is an induced harmonic graph to which the Section-123 payment applies,
`P_exc <= 10(e_BB-k)` then yields the conditional source-level bound

\[
{e_{BB}-k\over D}\ge {4-\pi\over10}-o(1).
\tag{BI.14}
\]

For the canonical **full positive-contact core** in the no-container Type-II branch,
the full-area hypothesis is in fact a consequence of the existing near-disk stack.
All core vertices lie in the loaded force hull `F` (loaded vertices are in `F`, and
each unloaded component is trapped in its attachment cell inside `F`). Hence the
bounded-face union `U` satisfies

\[
A_4\le \operatorname{area}(U)\le\operatorname{area}(F).
\]

Canonical full-core near-disk stability gives
`A_4/D^2 -> pi/4` and `area(F)/D^2 -> pi/4`, so squeezing yields
`area(U)/D^2 -> pi/4`. Therefore (BI.12)--(BI.14) apply to that full core whenever
the stated global two-phase and outer-walk hypotheses hold. This does **not** promote
the conclusion to an arbitrary selected harmonic carrier: its bounded-face union need
not carry the global near-disk area.

For a graph where only strict-rhombus area saturation is known, one must use (BI.9),
which already contains `P_exc`. Combining (BI.9) with (BI.10) gives instead

\[
\boxed{
2{P_{exc}\over D}+M-\pi
\ge {4-sM\over2-s}-o(1).
}
\tag{BI.15}
\]

At `M=pi`, this yields only

\[
P_{exc}\ge {4-\pi\over2}D-o(D),
\qquad
{e_{BB}-k\over D}\ge {4-\pi\over20}-o(1),
\tag{BI.16}
\]

when the Section-123 payment applies. The stronger (BI.13)--(BI.14) conclusion
requires the full-core area sandwich above (or another explicit full bounded-face
area hypothesis); it does not follow from strict-rhombus saturation for an arbitrary
graph alone.

This is a bridge-term removal, not a C-II closure. The near-geodesic full-area and
global two-phase hypotheses are not available for every current Type-II survivor;
the canonical obstruction remains the aggregate cell detour coupled to the loaded
diameter graph.

## 4. Scope and classification

- Bridges internal to bounded faces are not subtracted.
- Every source point remains present; no contact-isolated point is assigned a contact
  velocity or deleted.
- The proof uses only plane incidence, unit edge lengths, and ordinary planar
  isoperimetry.
- `U` is the full bounded-face union of the graph under discussion. A selected
  harmonic carrier need not have `A_D=pi D^2/4-o(D^2)`.
- (BI.8)--(BI.10) are conditional compositions. They do not produce a strict
  diameter-decreasing motion or an exact unit equilateral triangle.

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting hostile audit; proof-only. C-II remains
open.
